<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Jarrahids</h1><p class="st-meta">970 CE – 1107 CE (approx.) · capital Ramla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bedouin emirs of the Tayy tribe who dominated Palestine around Ramla and for a time challenged Fatimid rule by backing a rival caliph.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Jarrah, a leading clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a>, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in the later 10th century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> struggled to control <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. Their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mufarrij-ibn-daghfal">Mufarrij ibn Daghfal</a> alternated between serving and fighting the Fatimids and by the 980s held much of the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Jarrahids ruled as tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, collecting revenues and protection payments rather than building a state administration. In 1011-1013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mufarrij-ibn-daghfal">Mufarrij</a> proclaimed the sharif of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-futuh-al-hasan-ibn-jafar">Abu'l-Futuh</a>, as an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> before making peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. His son Hassan joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mirdasid-emirate">Mirdasids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> in a 1024 alliance to partition <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> defeated it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-uqhuwana">al-Uqhuwana</a> in 1029.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Hassan and his followers took service with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. The family later regained influence among the Syrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadl-ibn-rabia">Fadl ibn Rabi'a</a> switched between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in the early 12th century, and his descendants led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-fadl">Al Fadl</a> tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> times.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 970 CE – 1107 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.00%;width:2.73%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>970 CE – 1107 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ramla</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mufarrij ibn Daghfal, Hassan ibn Mufarrij, Fadl ibn Rabi'a</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Jarrah</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=jarrahids" aria-label="Jarrahids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5578 1016 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jarrahids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5898 1272L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 1272h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 1272h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ramla. <a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=jarrahids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

